we begin this newshour with breaking  news from the conflict in ethiopia where  tplf fighters appear to be on the back  foot in the amhara and afar regions  government troops have recorded several  victories in recent weeks after the  prime minister abi ahmed went to the  front line to lead the army government  troops now saying they've re-taken  control of the strategic towns of deci  and combulture from tigran rebels the  fighters had captured the towns in the  region of amhara in the past two months  let's get martin platt's thoughts on  this story he is a horn of africa  analyst and a senior research fellow at  the university of london he joins us on  skype from there martin platt welcome  back to the newshour how significant is  it that they've retaken these two towns  oh it's very significant i mean the the  tigrans have been pushed a long way back  i mean they have really been pushed back  through towns and villages that they  fought extremely hard to take and they  must have lost many lives in order to  capture them but one thing is important  to realize is that they withdrew they  were not forced out they withdrew with  their troops and much of their equipment  and this means that they possibly can  continue fighting in another location  now i have no idea where that might be  since uh they sweated a lot and lost a  lot of blood to get these towns but  clearly they have now gone does that  mean that they might be planning on some  sort of regrouping over the coming weeks  and months  that's exactly what they're saying  they're saying this was a strategic  withdrawal  rather than being defeated on the  battlefield  now whether that is true or not i'm not  in a position to say and one of the  great problems with this war is that  there are no independent journalists  allowed into tigre to report on what is  going on neither al jazeera nor the bbc  reuters anybody else is allowed to go up  to the front lines so we all have to  rely on outside on reports by the  combatants  there were  areas of speculation i guess martin what  two months ago now up until four weeks  ago people were saying well the tigran  rebels might even have the audacity to  sort of stage a march on the capital on  addis ababa addis ababa now is safe and  secure yeah  at the moment yes that is certainly what  it looks like uh they have withdrawn uh  you know a long way this is you know a  very substantial change in in the  situation and i think the reason is  quite simple uh it it looked as if the  uh ethiopian army was going to be able  to attack them from the flank from the  east and this would have cut their  forces in half and therefore they were  left with really few options but to  withdraw now what they will do from now  i don't know i mean the tigrans are  still a long way in outside of tigre  proper they're in parts of amhara and  the amhara region so what they're going  to do i i don't know and they're not  making their their views known uh i mean  the other thing that really changed  things was the drones which were flown  in via the uae from uh turkey from china  and that seems to have played a major  role in in this victory by the ethiopian  military but the central issues that  have led to this conflict those issues  have not gone away have they  in no way i mean the uh  and both sides are really still fighting  to the end i mean the other thing that's  happened today there's been a a major um  initiative by the international  community i think six seven countries  from australia canada denmark  netherlands united kingdom the united  states have all appealed to the uh  participants of the war to sit down and  negotiate i don't see that happening i  mean everyone has far too much to lose  by uh sitting down negotiating but maybe  i'm wrong maybe there are things going  on uh you have uh former president  ambassador of nigeria is the african  union mediator he said that he's made  quite a lot of progress maybe we'll now  see it on the table but uh i i wouldn't  hold the breath okay we'll leave it  there martin many thanks martin platt  good to talk to you again martin martin  platt here from the university of london
 

